(The prelude manifest.

 Josj, 2026-08-21: "Make everything random unless there is a rule to say
 otherwise. When in doubt, just make it random."

 What this replaces. Until now the prelude was a TEMPLATE: four things
 called t1..t4 with fields x1/x2/g1/g2, two methods called 'made at' and
 'reflected across', four functions called f1..f4, four flags called
 fl1label/fl1count/fl1on/fl1scratch. Every leaf that touched any of them
 wrote the name as a literal, which is why the prelude could never move:
 change it and thirty leaves stop compiling. `scripts/invariants` over
 150 programs on the plain layout reported 118 findings at 100%
 presence, and nearly every one was that template - 30 identical lines,
 33 fixed identifiers, 44 never-exceeded bounds, 9 fixed orderings and
 the two "always exactly 4" counts.

 Not one of them had a citation. LANGUAGE.md does not say a program has
 four things, or any things; it puts no ceiling on function count or
 arity; it says `Parse flags.` is optional (LANGUAGE.md:4764) and that
 ordinary code may sit between flag schema declarations
 (LANGUAGE.md:4781). Every one of those 118 rows was an undeclared rule
 - CLAUDE.md's "real enemy" - baked invisibly into the shape of a leaf.

 So the prelude is now DRAWN, per program, and what it drew is recorded
 here in a manifest. A leaf no longer says "t4"; it asks the manifest
 for a thing that has a maker, a receiver and a number field, and if
 this program has none, the leaf stands aside for one that always works
 - the eligibility pattern 'gen leaf deep grid' and 'gen leaf stdin
 read' already use. No leaf may depend on the prelude having a
 particular shape.

 The shape of the manifest: PARALLEL LISTS, one entry per declaration,
 with each thing recording where its fields and methods start and how
 many it has. That is the same representation gen_buffers.vox uses for
 its claimed-word marks and scripts/invariants.vox uses for its report
 rows, and it was chosen over a list of things for one reason: Vox
 things are value types with fixed layout and no growable member, so a
 thing holding a variable number of fields would have to carry a list
 anyway. Flat lists plus a first/count pair per owner cost nothing and
 read the same.

 Names are BARE PHRASES here - "the survey marker", not "'the survey
 marker'". A phrase is written out through 'gen written name' and
 'gen possessive name' (gen_collections.vox), which are the two forms
 Vox needs and which are not the same string: a phrase with a space in
 it must be quoted, and the possessive puts its `'s` INSIDE the closing
 quote. Hand-verified against 0.4.7 - the probes are listed against each
 rule below.

 Every LANGUAGE.md line number in this file was re-derived against the
 manual as it stood on 2026-08-21 at 14:26 (5316 lines). The manual MOVED
 during this work - it grew by 76 lines and everything past about line
 800 shifted by 20 to 55 - so if a citation here lands on a code fence or
 the wrong sentence, the manual has moved again: search for the claim,
 do not trust the number. Several citations elsewhere in this repo are
 already stale for exactly that reason; see REPORT-PRELUDE.md.

 What the rules genuinely pin, all of it, each verified rather than
 assumed:

 - A thing needs at least one field. `A thing called sighting has.` is
   rejected: "A thing needs at least one field". So the per-thing field
   count starts at 1, and NOTHING else about it is pinned.
 - A field may be number, float, boolean, time, or any PREVIOUSLY
   DEFINED thing (LANGUAGE.md:985-987). text/list/map/buffer are
   deferred, so those five kinds are the whole space.
 - A thing's methods name their owner type, so a definition precedes
   them (LANGUAGE.md:1416). Verified that a thing may otherwise sit
   anywhere: definitions may be emitted all together and then all the
   methods, or each definition followed by its own methods - both
   compile, so which one a program gets is drawn.
 - Every declared member returns its owner (LANGUAGE.md:1444), a maker's
   first parameter is not the owner and a receiver's is
   (LANGUAGE.md:1590-1611), and the manifest is checked both ways
   (LANGUAGE.md:1472) - so an entry without a definition, or a
   definition without an entry, is a compile error. Both are emitted
   from the same manifest row, which is what keeps them in step.
 - A flag needs BOTH aliases. `is "--tag", it is a text.` is rejected:
   "Expected long flag alias string like '--number'". So the
   `"-x" or "--long"` shape is the language's, not ours.
 - Type names, function names and flag names share ONE global identifier
   space (LANGUAGE.md:1678), so every name this file hands out at top
   level must be unique. Field, method and parameter names live in a
   per-type member space or in a function's locals and are free to
   repeat across owners.

 Everything else varies: how many things, how many fields, which field
 types, whether a thing has methods and which kinds, how many functions,
 their arity and parameter types and whether they return, how many
 flags, their types and defaults and aliases, whether `Parse flags.` is
 written at all, and whether the flag declarations are contiguous.)

(--- vocabularies ---

 Each list is a PRIME number of words long, because names are spent out
 of it on a cycle with a drawn stride (see 'gen cycled word' in
 gen_collections.vox): a prime length makes every stride coprime with
 it, so the walk visits every word before repeating and two names in one
 program are distinct by construction.

 The three top-level pools - types, functions, flags - are disjoint from
 each other AND from gen_buffer_words (gen_buffers.vox) and 'the value
 nouns'/'the value verbs' (gen_collections.vox), because all five spend
 names into the one global identifier space and a collision is a compile
 error. tests/230_units.vox asserts that disjointness so it cannot rot.

 Field, method and parameter pools do not have to be disjoint from
 anything - a member space belongs to its type and a parameter to its
 function - but they are kept separate anyway, which costs nothing and
 removes a whole class of mistake.

 The parameter pool is much the largest, and for a measured reason. A
 program spends a parameter name per function parameter, per method
 parameter, per method local, per function local AND once per level of
 grid-sink depth - which reaches 17 - so 25 to 30 names out of one pool
 is ordinary. With 29 words in it, every program spent nearly all of
 them and the invariant report came back with thirty rows reading "76%
 of programs use the identifier `share`". A pool a program uses a
 quarter of leaves nothing above the threshold. Sameness measured, not
 assumed: the first draft of this file had the small pool and the
 report said so.

 Single-word entries are checked against the keyword tables at
 LANGUAGE.md:4997-5689: none is a reserved word, and none is a
 contextual keyword either, since a contextual keyword is only claimed
 in its own grammatical position and a bare declaration name is not one.
 They matter more than the phrases do, because a phrase with a space is
 always written quoted and a quoted name is never read as a keyword.)

a list called gen_type_words is ["sighting", "the sighting", "waypoint", "the waypoint", "measurement", "the measurement", "bearing", "the bearing", "landmark", "the landmark", "the survey marker", "the tide mark", "coordinate", "the coordinate", "milestone", "the milestone", "the anchorage", "the moorings", "beacon", "the beacon", "the fix", "sounding", "the sounding", "the datum point", "the station", "the trig point", "the boundary stone", "cairn", "the cairn", "the survey plot", "the field note"].

a list called gen_field_words is ["span", "reach", "drift", "depth", "height", "width", "rise", "fall", "offset", "the north offset", "the east side", "the tide height", "the depth in fathoms", "the bearing in degrees", "the paces walked", "the chain length", "the run", "the drop", "the lie", "the sweep", "the arc", "the gradient", "the elevation", "the aspect", "the datum", "the delta", "the spread", "the margin", "the clearance", "the freeboard", "the draught"].

(Makers and receivers get separate pools so a thing that has both can
